 if you do  the optimization  of all of them together and two  possibilities  to use them as machine learning features  would be  first you just do a separate treatment  of each  category so this is basically what uh  what you see on in the upper plot so  there we have uh  here one one category for each  for each item number and you would see  here again the target mean  uh and everything in in every bin um  and uh doing so basically means that you  need uh the capability of a method to  deal with high cardinality  of such categorical features another  possibility would be  that after the target encoding  you would actually put everything to a  single variable  so that means you use the the target  means in each bin  and just put them in one single variable  which you see in the lower plot  so this can then can also be instead of  the mean  uh to include the trend for example you  could also use an exponential weighted  moving average for this  so of course always looking only in the  past of this productive patient  combination  the two of them are preferable in  different situations  so basically what you see is that  the separate treatment has a higher  variance but a lower bias  so and vice versa for the uh for the  mean  encoding and it depends on the situation  in which you are in  so usually you would say if you have not  much external  information available it is it is safe  to jump on the mean encoding method  because it reduces the  variance and there is not so much to  learn from from the other features  and in retail it is often preferable to  actually use a separate treatment  because you can make use of the  correlations with all other external  features  so in the end it is very important that  you think about it and treat your  categorical features well they are very  important  okay so now we go a bit beyond the pure  machine learning and  look into possible applications for  demand  shaping rather than forecasting so that  means  i mentioned before that uh statistical  learning and machine learning is just  curve fitting in many dimensions  uh if you really want to tell something  about the causal structure in your data  you need for an add-on to machine  learning so machine learning and data  are not enough  to describe the causal structure and  such an add-on is usually a causal  assumption  so what we end what we want in the end  is we want a method that allows us  to predict something that is called  counterfactuals  which is the fundamental problem of  causal inference  which asks what could have happened  instead so what  what is the issue is that you are we has  usually only one  uh one realization and you cannot  observe different potential outcomes  and what you want is you want uh to have  the individual causal effect between  such different outcomes  so one example for this would be a  demand shaping wire price setting  so this is a such an exact causal  what-if scenario when you say  the price was two dollar two dollars but  what would have happened to the demand  if the price would have been three  dollars  and of course you want this forward  looking  so that means yeah you somehow need need  the method that  enables you to do so and machine  learning is one  ingredient to this because it provides  you with the individualization  via its generalization capabilities and  the other ingredient  is deconfounding so for this let's first  look what  what i mean with confounding so in the  diagram  uh on the upper diagram here you could  see uh  three random variables so x would be for  example the price  that you can actually set so you have  you have the possibility  to to change this feature and y would be  your target which is the demand which  you want to influence so that means you  want to  to know the causal effect from x on y  but the issue now is that there are  other variables depicted as set here  which would have an influence both on x  and y so let's say for example the day  of the week  would have an effect on the demand  because more people go  go shopping on weekends but it might  also have an effect on the historical  data that you have from price setting  because  you just reduced the price more often on  the weekend for example  so and you want you want to get rid of  the influence from set on x here to  actually make uh  make a statement about the causal effect  from x and y  so and there are different methods that  you could use so the easiest one is of  course  a randomized control trials so that  means if you can set the price  randomly you would get rid of this  dependency of  saturn x but often you are left with  observational data and you don't uh  you have actually no chance to do so so  then  what what is what is another possibility  is that you use independence weights by  means of propensity scores that are  potentially learned from another machine  learning model  um and so getting so getting by doing so  rid of the of the dependency in your  observational data  um but you could also use some partial  deconfounding so this is sometimes good  enough  and uh smoothing that i mentioned before  can be used to partially impose causal  assumptions so an example is the lower  plot here  which shows you uh day after week a day  of the year  as as feature so you see the seasonality  and  what the smoothing function is here is  basically a sign  so you would not allow for wiggles in  such a smoothing function and you see  lots of  fluctuations of the data also spikes may  be coming from promotions or so  but you would not allow them to be  learned by this feature by just  having a smoothing function of a sign  here so this would be a causal  assumption  uh that this feature or could not affect  more  and this is often uh enough uh to  actually uh  have a bit of causal assumptions in your  method to  to just pimp it to do something like  demand shaping  okay besides the implications on causal  decision making a special kind of  confounding can also affect the  effectiveness  of your demand time series forecasting  itself  and i call this temporal confounding  here in the upper diagram you could see  what i mean so  y again is the demand and you look at  the demand at time t  so let's say today and at time t minus  one let's say yesterday  so what you usually have with  autocorrelations is that you have  a statistical dependency between  yesterday and today  but this is actually at least the bulk  of it is not  a causal effect so this is actually a  spurious correlation coming from the  fact  that you have a confounding with all the  other causal effects on demand  so for example if there's there was a  promotion yesterday there is a good  chance that there is also promotion  today  or if yesterday was summer there is  still summer today  so that means you have a you have a  common cause  for for the two for the two times here  uh which leads to a torque relay  correlation between the two  and this autocorrelation is actually  what is used by most state-of-the-art  time series forecasting methods  so including uh recurrent neural  networks for example so they basically  you  use select target information to do most  of the learning  of course they also include might also  include all the  uh the external features but the  learning of the effects especially uh  of of peaking structures is much harder  if you have the the leg target  information overlaid  so that means if you want to  uh to improve things like explainability  of your model mid and long term  forecasting quality which is not relying  too much on autocorrelation or the  predictability of  rare spiking events it is usually better  to strive for direct learning of the  causal effects  and an easy way to do so is you just not  using  a leg target information in your machine  learning model  so you just drop it from your model so  but then of course you might lose  information because  here you have all kind of hidden  information in that you might not have  in your other features  so to include it again you can just use  a subsequent residual correction  on the finest granularity so that means  on product location or granularity  so this you can do to capture all kind  of missing trends so for example why are  an exponential moving average  over the deviation between between  the predictions and the actual sales you  could see an example  in the lower plot here so this is about  the beginning of the covet crisis this  year  in yellow you could see the sales which  is suddenly rising for this product  group  at some point and you see that the  machine learning method in blue which is  not including lag target information  would not  uh would not predict this but the dashed  line which is after the residual  correction  would uh would just react to it in kind  of an online learning  uh after the first days where it would  see a deviation between the two  so in the end my advice is separate the  autocorrelation this can help a lot  to learn specific effects  so now we come to an interesting little  effect that cannot easily  be treated with a single machine  learning model and this is the direct  influence of one sample on another  so the famous use case in demand  forecasting here is cannibalization and  halo effects so what does this mean  so cannibalization means that the demand  of a product  decreases due to another product for  example in promotion at the same time  you could see this this behavior in the  plot  so here in the upper plot you see a  product that  has a huge sales increase when it comes  to promotion  which is uh which is visualized by the  by the red  periods and there might be another  product uh the lower plot  that has a quite stable sales behavior  but is actually dropping when this other  product goes to promotions because it's  losing sales to this other product it's  get  cannibalized by it so um this  you could actually do with an additional  machine learning  model after your main model so you have  a main model that gets you  the predictions including all other  effects  and then you look at the residuals as  targets  and what you do is you learn individual  parameter for each product pair  kind of a product form  and you only use pairs where you where  one is in promotion so you need kind of  a trigger event  this does not have to be promotion it  can also be another trigger like for  example an out of stock situation  and in the end you also want to have  some feature selection to avoid  overfitting in such a  such a product matrix form and this can  be a quite good method to capture such  effects  so actually employ residuals in such  cases  okay so an uh ever more uh important  aspect of machine learning predictions  is trust especially when it comes to  business critical use cases  so um what you what you want to have in  the end is an individual explainability  this is visualized in in the plot here  so what you could see is is a time  series  where you see the ingredients of a  prediction  so the different factors shown here show  you the different features of such a  model so for example the price events  weather and so on  and you see their behavior over time so  that means you see what  make up the prediction of an individual  product location combination  at each point in time so this shows for  example that  at the first four days in this plot  there was maybe a reduced price which  which drove the demand high and then  it's dropping for example and you could  also see the weekly structure overlaid  in the calendar factor here for example  so a suction individual explainability  uh  helps of course a lot to uh to dig into  root case  root causes for uh for for example um  too high predictions or something like  that so um  one one famous method to get there  is uh generalized additive models  so um and what what is also uh  useful is if you use a multiplicative  model here  so you could see it in the formula what  i mean by that so this is again  uh with the former method of binning um  and such a multiplicative model can come  quite handy because then you directly  get the different features as factors  like in the plot  um and uh usually a multiplicative  approach is also better than an additive  approach for many features because  it helps you to learn the right level of  uncertainty so basically  if the variation is proportional to the  level it's better to  to have a multiplicative model so in the  end what is important to note is that  you should not confuse such an  explainability with the causal structure  so this would actually have you would  have to employ some causal assumptions  this year would only give you  the inner workings of the model so  definitely you should try to build trust  with whitebox ml however you do it  okay so far we have only spoken about  point estimations but in fact  what you really want are predictions of  full probability density functions  so because these are crucial for  subsequent  probabilistic decision making so for  example uh by minimizing a cost function  so let's say you have different risks so  in a retail setting  for optimization this might be a stock  out risks or overstock risks  so and by having a full probability  density function like in the plot here  you could choose a low quantile of the  pdf for ordering  and would have low over stock risks but  hide out of stock risks  and vice versa for high quantiles  so and by by knowing your costs you  could actually use this  to optimize your cost function optimally  so there are different ways to get to  such a pdf so one would be a parametric  approach  where your demand is modeled according  to some  pdf assumption so this uh usually a  negative binomial is a good choice for  for retail cases so this would account  for over dispersion compared to a  possible distribution for example  and you would you would best use  different machine learning models for  both mean and variance  and having that mean and variance and  the causal assumption you would have the  full pdf  an alternative would be an empiric  density estimation  so for example via a quantile regression  so this is of course more flexible but  it's also less robust than a pdf  estimation  so then if you have such a method you of  course also want to check the goodness  of your pdf prediction in the end  so that means you want to evaluate them  and here i just  give a few suggestions how you would do  it so uh  on the left side you see you see a pdf  which you could convert then in  accumulated distribution function  and then you can actually look at the at  the actuals compared to your prediction  which is the dashed vertical line  so you get cdf observations so to say  and by by having such single and cdf  observations you could not tell much  about  the goodness of your pdf estimation but  by looking at  many of them together so for example by  filling them to an histogram you could  come to a conclusion  so such a histogram you would expect to  be flat so that means a uniform  distribution and any deviation from it  would actually hint to some flaws in  your method  so this you could also do in a  quantitative evaluation  by means of of looking at deviations  from the uniform distribution in terms  of for example the earth mover distance  or  coolback libra diversions or something  like that  and you can go even one step further in  the direction of qualitative evaluation  by not looking at the histogram itself  by actually looking at profile plots  according to some other variables so in  the case on the right plot here  you have the the mean prediction on the  x-axis  and you have different quantiles on the  y-axis where you  just compare your cdf observations in a  lower higher perspective  and by doing so you could you could see  first deviations for example in low  quantiles or in high quantiles  but you could also see deviations in  specific areas of the variable you are  looking at the x-axis  so this can be very helpful to really  pinpoint  to some to some flaws in your method  okay and that's it for today so of  course there are many more aspects  to this extensive topic but i hope that  you had some fun with the 10 selected  and  thanks a lot for your attention
 

